prompt_template:
Create an editorial poster in the "Cobalt Pop Cutout Editorial" visual style.

Style priority: preserve these observable anchors before introducing new content: {STYLE_FIDELITY_ANCHORS}
Do not reproduce any of this literal source content: {SOURCE_CONTENT_TO_AVOID}

Subject: {SUBJECT}
Action: {SUBJECT_ACTION}
Prop or visual anchor: {PRODUCT_OR_PROP}
Location: {LOCATION}
Background details: {BACKGROUND_ELEMENTS}
Main headline: {MAIN_TEXT}
Supporting microcopy: {SECONDARY_TEXT}
Accent symbol: {ACCENT_SYMBOL}
Wardrobe: {WARDROBE_STYLE}

Build a sparse, high-impact hybrid photo-and-graphic poster. Fill nearly the entire frame with a saturated cobalt-to-sky-blue field. Place one crisp photoreal full-body subject centrally at 55 to 70 percent of the frame height, photographed from a low 24 to 30 millimeter equivalent viewpoint with mild upward perspective and believable anatomy. Use one foreground prop low in the frame to create depth.

Set one enormous warm-orange headline across the upper third or upper half in chunky, solid-fill, hand-warped uppercase display lettering with swollen and tapered strokes, uneven widths, and an irregular baseline. Let the subject overlap selected letters while keeping the short headline inferable. Add only a few tiny cobalt registration tabs or short rules on letter edges.

Introduce two to four broad, flat botanical-green ribbons or amoebic blobs from the outer edges as an organic frame; they must not cover the face, main gesture, or headline. Add one compact warm-cream sans-serif microcopy block and no more than three tiny cream arrow, chevron, rule, or sparkle accents. Maintain a palette near 60 percent blue, 18 percent green, 17 percent orange, and 5 percent cream. Keep solid graphic fills clean and let visible texture come chiefly from the sharply graded daylight photograph.

For a 4:5 image, emphasize a tall central stack and a strong bottom crop. For a 5:4 image, widen the headline and use the side margins for sparse callouts while retaining one centered subject. The mood is confident, playful, colorful editorial street culture with clean digital compositing. Match the visual system first, then change the subject, action, prop, setting, text, and story.

Avoid: no source identity or wording, no fire hydrant, no seated head-in-hand pose, no crowded collage, no multiple people, no torn paper, no ransom type, no halftone, no photocopy grit, no heavy film grain, no paint splatter, no spray paint, no 3D render, no illustration-only subject, no extreme fisheye, no soft cinematic bokeh, no muted palette, no rainbow colors, no logos, no watermark, no username, no QR code, and no signature.
